qsm-console-guest -- the QSM Direct graphical console inside an LXC container
=============================================================================

Installed into a Debian/Ubuntu container with a KDE Plasma desktop by the host
tool 'qsm-pve-direct-lxc enable <vmid>' (package qsm-pve-direct-lxc on the
Proxmox VE node), which also passes the GPU through and enables the "QSM
Direct" entry of the container's Console button.

Like the ttys of PVE's terminal console, the container runs one console per
tty (qsm-consoles.service starts them at boot):

  qsm-display@N.service      headless sway (user qsm-display), /run/qsm-display-N
  qsm-seat-keeper@N.service  keeps the display's input devices stable
  qsm-login@N.service        login screen (qsm-greeter), PAM check, the session
  qsm-session-N.service      (transient) one logged-in desktop session: a real
                             PAM/logind session on seat0

The host's media worker captures a console's display and injects input.
Each desktop session also runs qsm-clipboard-agent (Klipper <-> the browser
console's clipboard, over /run/qsm-login/clip-N, reachable by that user only).
There is no audio from the container yet.  A
container has no VT, so greetd/SDDM cannot run; an enabled SDDM is disabled.
Only the current client of a display -- its login screen or its logged-in user
-- may connect to it (a POSIX ACL on the display socket).  The default session
type offered by the login screen is in /etc/qsm-direct/session-type.
